{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>אֲשֶׁר בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים לַעֲשׂוֹת.</b> Since the Sabbath testifies to creation from nothing, and specifically to that creation [He] ceased from — but from creation of something from something He did not cease, for how many plantings take root and grow on the Sabbath. And all the creatures created during the six days of the making were created in order to make from them something from something, for all of them are like clay from which some thing is fashioned. Thus all the creatures produce something from something from the six days of the making onward. Therefore it says ״אֲשֶׁר בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים לַעֲשׂוֹת״ — that He created them in order to make from them something from something, each day's matter on its day. And by inference from this we learn that the Sabbath is a miracle attesting to creation from nothing specifically, for if not, how would this day differ from all other days?" "And as for why it was not said regarding the Sabbath, ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר\": because on all the days the night is the time of rest and the morning is the time of labor, as it says (Psalms 104:22) ״תִּזְרַח הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ יֵאָסֵפוּן״ and so on, ״יֵצֵא אָדָם לְפָעֳלוֹ״. But the day of the Sabbath is either entirely rest or entirely labor — if so there is no distinction in it between evening and morning, for with respect to ordinary labor it is entirely rest, and with respect to the toil of Torah it is entirely labor, for the day of the Sabbath was given to engage in it in Torah, as is said of it (Joshua 1:8) ״וְהָגִיתָ בּוֹ יוֹמָם וָלַיְלָה״. And as is found in the midrash (Tanna devei Eliyahu Rabbah, chapter 1). And perhaps it is on this account that it says ״כִּי בוֹ שָׁבַת מִכָּל מְלַאכְתּוֹ אֲשֶׁר בָּרָא\", that is, ordinary labor — ״לַעֲשׂוֹת״, in the hewing of the tree of life which is Torah, and in the hewing of the stone of the tablets, and it is easy to understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, 